End-to-End Android Mobile Application Security Testing Lab
Audience: Mobile application security learners and penetration
testers  
Platform: Windows host + Android Studio + Android Emulator + Kali
Linux VM  
Suggested duration: 5 days / 30--35 practical hours  
Difficulty: Beginner to advanced
1. Purpose and scope
This manual builds a repeatable Android security-testing environment
from the ground up. It covers Android Studio installation, SDK
configuration, virtual-device creation, ADB connectivity, APK
installation, reconnaissance, static analysis, dynamic observation,
network analysis and advanced tooling.
Authorisation and safety: Test only applications, emulators, APIs
and networks that you own or are explicitly authorised to assess. Use
intentionally vulnerable training applications and synthetic data. Do
not use real customer credentials, personal data or production accounts.
Learning outcomes
By completing the labs, learners should be able to:
Install and configure Android Studio and the Android SDK.
Create and operate an Android Virtual Device (AVD).
Connect to an emulator using ADB.
Inventory an application's package, permissions and components.
Extract and inspect APK files.
Perform initial static analysis with APKTool and JADX.
Observe runtime behaviour and logs.
Inspect authorised application traffic using Burp Suite and
Wireshark.
Use MobSF for automated analysis and validate its findings.
Understand the roles of Frida and Objection in advanced dynamic
analysis.
Produce an evidence-based mobile application security report.
---
2. Reference lab architecture
Recommended architecture
Windows host: Android Studio, Android SDK, Android Emulator and
AVD Manager.
Kali Linux VM: JADX, APKTool, ADB (optional), Burp Suite,
Wireshark, MobSF, Frida and Objection.
Android Emulator: A virtual Pixel device running a selected
Android release.
Network: A controlled host-only or explicitly configured virtual
network between Windows, Kali and the emulator.
The emulator should run on Windows in this environment because Kali
currently does not receive Intel VT-x (`vmx`) or `/dev/kvm`. Running the
emulator inside Kali may therefore be slow or unable to use hardware
acceleration.
``` text
                    WINDOWS HOST
+---------------------------------------------------+
| Android Studio                                    |
| Android SDK / Platform Tools / AVD Manager        |
|                                                   |
|              Android Emulator                    |
|              Android OS + Test APK               |
+-----------------------|---------------------------+
                        |
             Controlled virtual network
                        |
+-----------------------|---------------------------+
|                    KALI LINUX VM                  |
|                                                   |
| ADB | JADX | APKTool | MobSF | Burp Suite         |
| Frida | Objection | Wireshark | Evidence/Reports  |
+---------------------------------------------------+
```
Networking note
The Android Emulator special address `10.0.2.2` refers to the Windows
host from inside the emulator. It does not automatically refer to
Kali. For a proxy running in Kali, configure an address reachable from
the emulator and verify routing before configuring Burp. `127.0.0.1` on
Windows, Kali and the emulator refers to each environment itself.
