PART A --- WINDOWS AND ANDROID STUDIO SETUP
Lab 1 --- Verify Windows host readiness
Objective: Confirm that the host can run Android Studio and an
accelerated emulator.
1.1 Check Windows and hardware
Open PowerShell:
``` powershell
Get-ComputerInfo | Select-Object WindowsProductName, WindowsVersion, OsArchitecture
```
Check memory:
``` powershell
Get-CimInstance Win32_ComputerSystem |
  Select-Object @{Name="RAM_GB";Expression={[math]::Round($_.TotalPhysicalMemory / 1GB, 1)}}
```
Check CPU:
``` powershell
Get-CimInstance Win32_Processor |
  Select-Object Name, NumberOfCores, NumberOfLogicalProcessors
```
1.2 Check virtualization
Open Task Manager → Performance → CPU and inspect Virtualization.
Also run:
``` powershell
systeminfo.exe
```
If Windows reports that a hypervisor has been detected, Windows is
already running a hypervisor layer. This is not necessarily a problem
for the Windows Android Emulator; it can use Windows Hypervisor Platform
(WHPX) when configured and supported.
1.3 Check Windows Hypervisor Platform
Open Turn Windows features on or off and inspect Windows
Hypervisor Platform.
Do not routinely disable Hyper-V, Virtual Machine Platform, VBS or
Memory Integrity. Diagnose emulator errors before changing host security
settings.
1.4 Capacity planning
---
Resource                                             Suggested baseline
---
Host RAM                                     16 GB minimum for a modest
single-emulator workflow; 32 GB
preferred
Free disk                      30--50 GB for Studio, SDK, system images
and lab files
Emulator count                                        One AVD initially
Kali VM RAM                      2--4 GB initially; adjust to available
host RAM
Kali VM CPU                                           2 vCPUs initially
Do not allocate nearly all host memory to Kali while Android Studio and
the emulator are running.
Learner task: Record Windows version, CPU, RAM, free disk,
virtualization status and whether Windows Hypervisor Platform is
enabled. Save a screenshot of Task Manager → Performance → CPU.
