Lab 7 --- Application attack surface mapping
Objective: Identify application features, entry points, data flows
and exposed components.
7.1 Build a feature inventory
---
Feature        Input               Output         Data sensitivity    Follow-up test
---
Login          Username/password   Session/auth   High                Authentication
result                             review
Profile        User data           Profile        Medium/high         Authorization
details                            review
Search         Search term         Results        Context-dependent   Input validation
File feature   File path/content   File operation Context-dependent   Storage review
API feature    Request parameters  Server         Context-dependent   Communication
response                           review
Populate only features actually present in the target.
7.2 Inspect package details
``` bash
adb shell pm list packages -3
adb shell dumpsys package <PACKAGE_NAME>
```
Review package/version, requested permissions, activities, services,
receivers, providers, install location and application flags.
7.3 Identify APK path
``` bash
adb shell pm path <PACKAGE_NAME>
```
Pull the base APK:
``` bash
adb pull <RETURNED_APK_PATH> target.apk
```
Some applications use split APKs. If multiple paths are returned,
preserve and pull the relevant splits.
7.4 Component review
The manifest is the primary source for component declarations. Do not
conclude that an exported component is exploitable merely because it is
exported. Determine whether access is protected by permissions,
authentication, caller checks and input validation.
Learner challenge: Select one activity, service, receiver or
provider and document its name, exported status, required permission,
possible external interaction and evidence needed to validate
exploitability.
