PART D --- APK STATIC ANALYSIS
Lab 10 --- APK structure and metadata
Objective: Understand the APK format and extract metadata.
10.1 Create workspace
``` bash
mkdir -p ~/MobileSecurity/{APK,Decoded,JADX,Traffic,Reports,Evidence}
```
Copy the authorised APK into `~/MobileSecurity/APK/`, then:
``` bash
cd ~/MobileSecurity/APK
sha256sum target.apk
```
10.2 Inspect archive contents
``` bash
unzip -l target.apk | head -80
```
Look for:
`AndroidManifest.xml`
`classes.dex`
`resources.arsc`
`res/`
`assets/`
`lib/`
10.3 Inspect metadata
If Android Build Tools are installed:
``` bash
aapt dump badging target.apk
```
Review package identifier, version name/code, SDK information,
application label and launchable activity.
10.4 Identify DEX files
``` bash
unzip -l target.apk | grep -E 'classes.*dex'
```
DEX files contain Android bytecode and are a major source for
reverse-engineering analysis.
Learner task: Prepare an APK metadata report with hash, package,
version, SDK requirements and key archive entries.
---
Lab 11 --- Decode APK using APKTool
Objective: Decode the manifest and resources for review.
11.1 Verify APKTool
``` bash
apktool --version
```
If unavailable on Kali:
``` bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y apktool
```
11.2 Decode
``` bash
apktool d target.apk -o target_decoded
```
11.3 Review manifest
``` bash
less target_decoded/AndroidManifest.xml
```
Search key configuration:
``` bash
grep -nE 'uses-permission|activity|service|receiver|provider|exported|debuggable|allowBackup|usesCleartextTraffic' target_decoded/AndroidManifest.xml
```
11.4 Review resources and assets
``` bash
find target_decoded/res -maxdepth 2 -type f | head -60
find target_decoded/assets -type f 2>/dev/null
find target_decoded/lib -type f 2>/dev/null
```
Learner challenge: Identify three permissions, two components, one
application configuration setting and one resource/asset of security
interest. Record file paths and evidence.
---
Lab 12 --- Decompile with JADX
Objective: Review reconstructed Java-like source and identify areas
for further testing.
12.1 Verify and launch
``` bash
jadx --version
jadx-gui target.apk
```
Or export source:
``` bash
jadx -d jadx_output target.apk
```
12.2 Explore code
Look for:
Login/registration logic
API client classes
URL constants
Database helpers
File handling
Cryptographic routines
WebView usage
Logging
Authentication/session management
12.3 Search for investigation leads
``` bash
grep -RniE 'https?://|password|secret|token|api[_-]?key|Authorization' jadx_output
```
Search decoded resources too:
``` bash
grep -RniE 'https?://|password|secret|token|api[_-]?key' target_decoded
```
A matching string is not automatically a vulnerability. Inspect context
and determine whether a value is a real secret, test string, public
endpoint or unused resource.
12.4 Review WebView use
``` bash
grep -RniE 'WebView|setJavaScriptEnabled|addJavascriptInterface|loadUrl' jadx_output
```
Assess origin control, JavaScript settings, bridge exposure, navigation
handling and data exposed to the page.
12.5 Review logging
``` bash
grep -RniE 'Log\.|System\.out|printStackTrace' jadx_output
```
Inspect surrounding code to determine whether sensitive data could be
written to logs.
Learner challenge: Find three code locations needing further
testing. Record class/method, file path, relevant context, security
concern and dynamic validation required.
