Lab 16 --- Burp Suite proxy configuration
Objective: Route authorised emulator traffic through Burp and
inspect requests.
16.1 Start Burp
Open Burp Suite.
Create a temporary project.
Select Burp defaults.
Open Proxy → Proxy settings.
Inspect or create a listener on port `8080`.
16.2 Select the listener interface
If Burp runs in Kali and the emulator runs on Windows, the listener must
bind to an interface reachable from the emulator. Find Kali addresses:
``` bash
ip addr
ip route
```
Use the Kali IP on the shared controlled network. Do not bind to an
interface exposed to untrusted networks.
16.3 Configure emulator proxy
Use the emulator's network/proxy settings or Android proxy settings:
Open emulator extended controls if available.
Locate network/proxy settings.
Configure the reachable Kali host address and port `8080`.
Save and test against an approved endpoint.
16.4 Verify
Use the emulator browser to visit an approved test endpoint. Confirm the
request appears in Burp Proxy → HTTP history.
If no traffic appears, verify proxy address, listener binding, firewall,
emulator settings, routing and whether the application actually makes a
request.
16.5 HTTPS certificate considerations
Burp HTTPS inspection requires the client to trust Burp's CA. Android
versions and apps differ in trust behaviour.
Modern Android apps targeting newer API levels generally do not
trust user-installed CA certificates by default unless Network
Security Configuration permits it.
Some apps use certificate pinning.
Installing a CA does not automatically defeat pinning.
Do not disable pinning or modify an app unless explicitly authorised
and in scope.
For an introductory exercise, demonstrate HTTP history and HTTPS
connection metadata first. For HTTPS content inspection, use a
purpose-built training app configured to trust the lab CA or an approved
test build.
16.6 Inspect a request
For an approved request, inspect method, URL, headers, content type,
parameters, status, response headers/body and session behaviour. Redact
credentials and tokens from evidence.
Learner challenge: Capture one authorised request/response and
explain the data flow and two security questions requiring further
investigation.
