PART F --- ADVANCED ANALYSIS
Lab 18 --- MobSF static analysis
Objective: Generate an automated assessment and manually validate
selected findings.
Project: https://github.com/MobSF/Mobile-Security-Framework-MobSF
Use a trusted release and the project's current documented deployment
method. Do not expose MobSF to a public network.
18.1 Check Docker
``` bash
docker --version
docker compose version
```
If Docker is unavailable, follow current official Docker instructions
for your Kali/Debian release. Avoid unreviewed installation scripts.
18.2 Deploy MobSF
Follow the official MobSF repository's current Docker instructions. A
commonly documented quick-start pattern is:
``` bash
docker pull opensecurity/mobile-security-framework-mobsf
docker run -it --rm -p 8000:8000 opensecurity/mobile-security-framework-mobsf:latest
```
Use the current project instructions and pin a version for repeatable
delivery where possible. The `latest` tag is convenient but less
reproducible.
Open the local MobSF web interface at the address and port shown by the
deployment.
18.3 Upload and review
Upload the authorised training APK.
Wait for static analysis.
Review app metadata, permissions, manifest, network security, code
analysis, hardcoded strings, trackers, dependencies, cryptography
and storage indicators.
Export the report if available.
18.4 Validate findings
For each selected finding:
Locate the evidence.
Inspect it in JADX/APKTool.
Determine whether it applies.
Assess exploitability and impact.
Record false positives or limitations.
Recommend remediation.
Learner challenge: Select three MobSF findings and classify each as
confirmed, not applicable, inconclusive or requiring further dynamic
testing.
